  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Hot, dry, and smoky conditions continue through this week. \n\n- Isolated thunderstorms remain a threat for southwest Montana\n  including Butte. Potentially expanding northward late this week.\n\n- Elevated fire weather conditions by weekend due to hot and dry\n  conditions, with an increase in winds due to projected ridge\n  breakdown.\n\n- Cool down to near average temperatures Sunday, though it \n  remains dry.\n\nHot, dry, and smokey conditions continue this week. Southwest MT\nwhere smoke is not as thick remains unstable and daily, isolated,\ndry thunderstorms are possible. Beginning Friday a stationary\nnorth Pacific trough begins to strengthen and waves of energy are\nexpected to eject into the southwesterly flow over the Northern \nRockies. Saturday that trough moves inland and a dry cold front will\npush through our region late evening into Sunday. \n\nIn the near term, air quality and visibility issues from smoke\nwill be the main story. That said, isolated, dry thunderstorms \nwill continue to be possible each evening in southwest MT. \nErractic and strong outflows with lightning and little to no \nprecipitation are expected. Thursday evening a well defined wave \nof energy is expected to eject off the Pacific trough. The \natmosphere will be favorable, with strong mid-level lapse rates \nand decent shear available, for strong to severe thunderstorms. \nThere is a distinct lack of moisture which would indicate the \nthreat will be dry lightning and strong to severe outflow winds. \nThe wild card will be surface smoke density and how it effects \natmospheric stability. \n\nFriday through the weekend fire weather concerns become elevated\nas the ridge breakdown the weather models have been forecasting\ncomes into focus. Isolated locations, mainly southwest MT, will\nhave chances for gusts 20 to 30 mph on Friday. By Saturday, there\nare widespread chances for westerly wind gusts 30 to 40 mph and \novernight into Sunday the focus will be northwest MT where gusts \nto 50 mph are possible along the Continental Divide. \n\nIn addition to Fire WX concerns, those with outdoor plans\nespecially in NW MT should be prepared for strong winds Saturday \nevening into Sunday. Back country roads may become blocked by down\nbranches. \n\nTired of near constant 90s, the region will cool 8 to 12 degrees \nbehind the dry cold front. While the models predict the ridge to \nslowly rebuild over the region, enjoy those 3 or 4 days of \n'seasonably' cool weather.",
